<section xml:id="sec-if-statement">
	<title>If Statements</title>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-if-statement">
		<title>If Statement</title>

		<p>
			The simplest if-statement tests a single 
			<xref ref="sec-logical-expressions" text="custom">logical expression</xref> 
			and runs a block of code if the expression is true.
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="if-statement">
			<title>If Statement Syntax</title>

			<p>
				The basic structure of an if-statement is:
			</p>

			<program language="matlab" line-numbers="no">
				if condition
					block	% ⬅ runs if condition is true
				end
			</program>

			<p>
				where
				<ul marker="square">
					<li><pf>condition</pf> is a logical expression that evaluates to <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf>.</li>
					<li><pf>block</pf> is the block of code that runs if the condition is true.</li>
					<li>If <pf>condition</pf> is <pf>false</pf>, MATLAB does nothing and skips to <pf>end</pf>.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

		</assemblage>

		<example xml:id="ex-if-simple">
			<title>ID, Please</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					This script checks the age of a person entering a bar.
					<ol>
						<li>Run the code below, as is, to see the displayed message. </li>
						<li>Set <pf>age = 16</pf> and run it again.</li>
					</ol>
				</p>

				<sage language="octave">
					<input>
						age = 23;

						if age &gt;= 21
						  fprintf("Bouncer: You may enter the bar.\n");
						end
					</input>
				</sage>

				<p>
					Notice that when <pf>age = 23</pf>, the logical expression <pf>age &gt;= 21</pf> 
					is true, so MATLAB executes the <pf>fprintf</pf> message. 
					When <pf>age = 16</pf>, the logical expression <pf>age &gt;= 21</pf> 
					is false, so MATLAB skips the <pf>fprintf</pf> message entirely. 
				</p>
			</statement>
		</example>

		<exercise label="ckpt-the-if-statement">
			<title>Understanding the <pf>if</pf>-Statement</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					The following questions relate to the basic <pf>if</pf> statement:
				</p>

				<program language="matlab" line-numbers="no">
					if statement
						if_body
					end
				</program>
			</introduction>

			<task label="ckpt-the-if-statement-a">
				<title>What happens when the logical expression is false?</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What happens when the <pf>statement</pf> is false?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								MATLAB runs the <pf>if_body</pf> once and then stops the script.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								The <pf>if</pf> statement never stops execution by itself.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								MATLAB skips to the line after <pf>end</pf>.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								When the <pf>statement</pf> is false, MATLAB simply moves past the block.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								MATLAB runs both the <pf>if_body</pf> and the next line after <pf>end</pf>.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Only one path executes: either the block runs or it is skipped.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								MATLAB reports an error because the <pf>statement</pf> is false.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								False, a true or false <pf>statement</pf> is expected, so no error is raised.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="ckpt-the-if-statement-b">
				<title>Required keyword to close an if statement</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What keyword must appear at the end of every <pf>if</pf> statement?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								<c>stop</c>
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								MATLAB does not use <pf>stop</pf> to close an <pf>if</pf> statement.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								<c>end</c>
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Every <pf>if</pf> statement must be closed with <pf>end</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								<c>endif</c>
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								MATLAB uses <pf>end</pf> instead of <pf>endif</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								<c>done</c>
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								MATLAB does not use <pf>done</pf> to close an <pf>if</pf> statement.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="ckpt-the-if-statement-c">
				<title>If statement requires true or false condition</title>
				<statement correct="yes">
					<p>
						The <pf>statement</pf> must evaluate to either <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						The <pf>statement</pf> must evaluate to either <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf>, which determines whether the <pf>if_body</pf> executes.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

		</exercise>
	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-if-else-statement">
		<title>If-Else Statement</title>

		<p>
			The previous example runs one code block when the condition is true, but 
			what if you want to run a different block when the condition is false?
			This behavior requires an additional <pf>else</pf> block.
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="if-else-statement">
			<title>If-Else Statement Syntax</title>

			<p>
				The structure of an <pf>if-else</pf> statement is:
			</p>

			<program language="matlab" line-numbers="no">
				if condition     
					blockA	% ⬅ runs if condition is true
				else
					blockB	% ⬅ runs if condition is false
				end
			</program>

			<p>
				where
				<ul marker="square">
					<li><pf>condition</pf> is a logical expression that evaluates to <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf>.</li>
					<li><pf>blockA</pf> runs if the condition is true.</li>
					<li><pf>blockB</pf> runs if the condition is false.</li>
					<li>Exactly one of <pf>blockA</pf> or <pf>blockB</pf> runs, never both.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

		</assemblage>

		<example xml:id="ex-if-else">
			<title>ID, Please continued..</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Adding an <pf>else</pf> block to the
					<xref ref="ex-if-simple" text="custom">previous example</xref>
					allows the bouncer to respond to patrons of any age.
					<ol>
						<li>Run the code below, as is, to see the displayed message. </li>
						<li>Set <pf>age = 16</pf> and run it again.</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
				<sage language="octave">
					<input>
						age = 23;

						if age &gt;= 21
							fprintf("Bouncer: You may enter the bar.\n");
						else
							fprintf("Bouncer: You are not getting in.\n");
						end
					</input>
				</sage>
			</statement>
		</example>

		<exercise label="ckpt-if-else-statement">
			<title>Understanding the <pf>if</pf>-<pf>else</pf> Statement</title>

			<task label="ckpt-if-else-statement-a">
				<title>If-else executes exactly one branch</title>
				<statement correct="yes">
					<p>
						In an <pf>if-else</pf> statement, MATLAB executes exactly one of the two branches.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						Because the statement is either true or false, MATLAB runs the matching branch and skips the other.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="ckpt-if-else-statement-b">
				<title>When does the else branch run?</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						When does the <pf>else</pf> branch in an <pf>if-else</pf> statement execute?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								The <pf>else</pf> branch runs before the <pf>if</pf> branch.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								MATLAB checks the <pf>if</pf> condition first, then decides which branch to run.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								The <pf>else</pf> branch runs when the condition is false.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								The <pf>else</pf> branch is the alternative path that executes when the condition evaluates to false.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								The <pf>else</pf> branch runs after the <pf>if</pf> branch completes.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Only one branch runs; they never both execute in the same pass through the code.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								The <pf>else</pf> branch never runs.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								The <pf>else</pf> branch runs whenever the condition is false.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="ckpt-if-else-statement-c">
				<title>If-else provides two mutually exclusive paths</title>
				<statement correct="yes">
					<p>
						An <pf>if-else</pf> statement is useful when you need to choose between two mutually exclusive actions.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						The <pf>if-else</pf> structure guarantees that exactly one of two code paths executes based on whether the condition is true or false.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>
		</exercise>

	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-if-elseif-else-statement">
		<title>If-ElseIf-Else Statement</title>

		<p>
			Sometimes, you want to run one of several different blocks of code based on multiple conditions.
			This requires the addition of one or more <pf>elseif</pf> blocks, depending on how many 
			conditions you need to check. 
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="if-elseif-else-statement-structure">
			<title>If-ElseIf-Else Statement Syntax</title>

			<p>
				The structure of an <pf>if-elseif-else</pf> statement is:
			</p>

			<program language="matlab" line-numbers="no">
				if conditionA
					blockA	% ⬅ runs if conditionA is true
				elseif conditionB
					blockB	% ⬅ runs if conditionA is false &amp; conditionB is true
				elseif conditionC
					blockC 	% ⬅ runs if conditionA &amp; B are false &amp; conditionC is true
				else
					blockD	% ⬅ runs if all statements above are false (default)
				end
			</program>

			<p>
				where
				<ul marker="square">
					<li>each <pf>condition</pf> is a logical expression that evaluates to <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf>.</li>
					<li>each <pf>condition</pf> is checked in order from top to bottom.</li>
					<li>exactly one of the blocks will run when the code executes.</li>
					<li>the <pf>else</pf> block runs only when all previous conditions are false.</li>
					<li>the <pf>else</pf> block is optional.</li>
					<li>you may add as many <pf>elseif</pf> blocks as needed.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

		</assemblage>

		<example xml:id="ex-if-elseif">
			<title>If-ElseIf-Else Statement</title>
			<statement>
				<p>Assign a letter grade based on a numeric score.</p>
				<program language="matlab">
					score = 82;
					if score >= 90
					grade = 'A';
					elseif score >= 80
					grade = 'B';
					elseif score >= 70
					grade = 'C';
					else
					grade = 'F';
					end
					fprintf('Grade: %s\n', grade)
				</program>
				<p>Since 82 falls in the 80–89 range, the output is: <c>Grade: B</c></p>
			</statement>
		</example>

		<exercise label="ckpt-if-elseif-else-statement">
			<title>Understanding the if-elseif-else Statement</title>

			<task label="ckpt-if-elseif-else-statement-a">
				<title>How elseif chains work</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Which statements about an <pf>if-elseif-else</pf> chain are true? Select all that apply.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								Conditions are checked from top to bottom.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								MATLAB evaluates logical expressions in order and stops at the first true one.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								The <pf>else</pf> block runs even if a previous logical expression is true.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								The <pf>else</pf> block is a fallback and only runs when all logical expressions are false.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								Only the first true logical expression's block executes.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Once MATLAB finds a true logical expression, it skips the remaining branches.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								You must include an <pf>else</pf> branch in every chain.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								The <pf>else</pf> branch is optional, though it can be helpful as a default.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								You can include multiple <pf>elseif</pf> branches.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Add as many <pf>elseif</pf> tests as you need to cover different cases.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="ckpt-if-elseif-else-statement-b">
				<title>Order matters in elseif chains</title>
				<statement correct="yes">
					<p>
						In an <pf>if-elseif-else</pf> chain, changing the order of logical expressions can change which block executes.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						Because MATLAB checks logical expressions from top to bottom and stops at the first true one, the order of statements matters. If multiple logical expressions could be true, only the first true statement's block will execute.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="ckpt-if-elseif-else-statement-c">
				<title>Purpose of the else branch</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What is the purpose of the <pf>else</pf> branch in an <pf>if-elseif-else</pf> chain?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								To check one more logical expression before ending.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								The <pf>else</pf> branch does not check a logical expression; use <pf>elseif</pf> to check additional logical expressions.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								To provide a default action when no logical expressions are true.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								The <pf>else</pf> branch serves as a fallback that runs when all previous logical expressions evaluate to false.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								To close the <pf>if</pf> statement.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								The <pf>end</pf> keyword closes the <pf>if</pf> statement, not <pf>else</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								To run code after all other branches have executed.
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Only one branch executes; the <pf>else</pf> runs instead of the others, not after them.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

		</exercise>

	</subsection>

	<reading-questions xml:id="if-statement-quiz">
		<title>📖 Reading Quiz</title>
		<exercise label="concepts-if-statement">
			<title>Answer the Following Questions Related to If-Statements</title>

			<task label="cqs-if-statement-if-end">
				<title>If Statement Requirements</title>
				<statement correct="yes">
					<p>
						Every <pf>if</pf> statement in MATLAB must end with the keyword <pf>end</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						Correct! The <pf>end</pf> keyword is required to close every <pf>if</pf> statement, 
						whether it contains just an <pf>if</pf> block, an <pf>if-else</pf>, or an <pf>if-elseif-else</pf> chain.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-if-statement-else-optional">
				<title>Else Branch Requirement</title>
				<statement correct="no">
					<p>
						An <pf>else</pf> branch is required in every <pf>if</pf> statement.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						Incorrect. The <pf>else</pf> branch is optional. If you only need to run code when a logical expression 
						is true, you can use just an <pf>if</pf> block without an <pf>else</pf>.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-if-statement-logical expression-order">
				<title>Elseif Chain Evaluation Order</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						In an <pf>if-elseif-else</pf> chain, how does MATLAB determine which block to execute?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="no">
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>MATLAB checks all logical expressions and runs the block with the highest priority.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. MATLAB evaluates logical expressions in order from top to bottom and stops 
								at the first true statement.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>MATLAB checks logical expressions from top to bottom and runs the first true block.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! Once MATLAB finds a true logical expression, it executes that block and skips all remaining branches.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>MATLAB checks all logical expressions and runs all blocks whose statements are true.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. Only one block executes in an <pf>if-elseif-else</pf> chain, 
								even if multiple logical expressions are true.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>MATLAB evaluates logical expressions randomly.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. Logical expressions are always evaluated in the order they appear in the code.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-if-statement-multiple-branches">
				<title>How Many Blocks Execute?</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Given this code:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						x = 10;
						if x &gt; 5
							y = 1;
						elseif x &gt; 8
							y = 2;
						elseif x &gt; 3
							y = 3;
						else
							y = 0;
						end
					</program>
					<p>
						What is the value of <pf>y</pf> after this code executes?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="no">
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p><pf>y = 2</pf></p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The first logical expression (<pf>x &gt; 5</pf>) is true, so MATLAB 
								never checks the second logical expression.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p><pf>y = 3</pf></p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The first logical expression (<pf>x &gt; 5</pf>) is true, so MATLAB 
								never checks the third logical expression.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p><pf>y = 6</pf> (sum of 1+2+3)</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. Only one block executes in an <pf>if-elseif-else</pf> chain. 
								The blocks don't accumulate.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p><pf>y = 1</pf></p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! Even though <pf>x &gt; 8</pf> and <pf>x &gt; 3</pf> are also true, 
								MATLAB stops at the first true logical expression (<pf>x &gt; 5</pf>) and assigns <pf>y = 1</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-if-statement-nested-vs-chain">
				<title>Nested If vs Elseif Chain</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What is the main difference between using nested <pf>if</pf> statements and using an <pf>if-elseif</pf> chain?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="no">
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>There is no difference; they are equivalent in all cases.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. Nested <pf>if</pf> statements can check multiple conditions independently, 
								while <pf>elseif</pf> chains are mutually exclusive.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>Nested <pf>if</pf> statements run faster than <pf>elseif</pf> chains.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. Performance is not the key difference; the distinction is in the logic structure 
								and whether branches are mutually exclusive.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>Nested <pf>if</pf> statements can allow multiple blocks to execute; 
							<pf>elseif</pf> chains execute only one block.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! In an <pf>if-elseif-else</pf> chain, only one block runs. 
								With nested <pf>if</pf> statements, multiple independent conditions can be true.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>Elseif chains require more <pf>end</pf> keywords than nested <pf>if</pf> statements.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. Actually, nested <pf>if</pf> statements require more <pf>end</pf> keywords 
								(one for each <pf>if</pf>), while an <pf>elseif</pf> chain needs only one.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-if-statement-condition-type">
				<title>If Statement Condition Type</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Consider this code:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						x = 5;
						if x
							disp('x is true')
						else
							disp('x is false')
						end
					</program>
					<p>
						What will be displayed, and why?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="no">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p><pf>x is true</pf>, because any non-zero number is treated as true.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! In MATLAB, non-zero values are treated as true in logical expressions, 
								and zero is treated as false.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p><pf>x is false</pf>, because <pf>x</pf> is not a logical value.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. While <pf>x</pf> is numeric, MATLAB automatically converts non-zero 
								numbers to true in logical expressions.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>An error occurs because <pf>x</pf> is not a logical value.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. MATLAB allows numeric values in <pf>if</pf> conditions and treats 
								non-zero as true and zero as false.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p><pf>x is false</pf>, because <pf>5</pf> is not equal to <pf>true</pf>.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. MATLAB doesn't compare <pf>x</pf> to <pf>true</pf>; it converts 
								non-zero values to true.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="chk-if-end">
				<statement><p>Every MATLAB <pf>if</pf> block must be closed with which keyword?</p></statement>
				<choices>
					<choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>end</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. <pf>end</pf> closes the if block.</p></feedback></choice>
					<choice><statement><p><pf>endif</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>MATLAB uses <pf>end</pf>, not <pf>endif</pf>.</p></feedback></choice>
					<choice><statement><p><pf>stop</pf></p></statement><feedback><p><pf>stop</pf> is not a MATLAB keyword for closing blocks.</p></feedback></choice>
					<choice><statement><p><pf>done</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>MATLAB uses <pf>end</pf> to close all control structures.</p></feedback></choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="chk-if-branches">
				<statement><p>In an if/elseif/else chain, how many branches execute when the first condition is true?</p></statement>
				<choices>
					<choice correct="yes"><statement><p>Only the first true branch executes.</p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. MATLAB evaluates top to bottom and executes only the first matching branch.</p></feedback></choice>
					<choice><statement><p>All true branches execute.</p></statement><feedback><p>No — once a true branch is found, the rest are skipped.</p></feedback></choice>
					<choice><statement><p>The last matching branch executes.</p></statement><feedback><p>MATLAB takes the first match, not the last.</p></feedback></choice>
					<choice><statement><p>Both the matching branch and the else branch execute.</p></statement><feedback><p>The else branch only runs when no condition is true.</p></feedback></choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="chk-if-false">
				<statement><p>In MATLAB, a condition is considered false when it evaluates to:</p></statement>
				<choices>
					<choice correct="yes"><statement><p>Zero (0).</p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. Zero is false; any nonzero value is true.</p></feedback></choice>
					<choice><statement><p>The string <pf>'false'</pf>.</p></statement><feedback><p>MATLAB uses numeric 0/1 for logical values, not strings.</p></feedback></choice>
					<choice><statement><p>An empty matrix <pf>[]</pf>.</p></statement><feedback><p>An empty matrix is also treated as false, but the most common false value is 0.</p></feedback></choice>
					<choice><statement><p>A negative number.</p></statement><feedback><p>Any nonzero number — positive or negative — is true in MATLAB.</p></feedback></choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="chk-if-elseif-chain">
				<statement><p>In an if/elseif/else chain, how many branches execute when the first condition is true?</p></statement>
				<choices>
					<choice correct="yes"><statement><p>Only the first true branch executes.</p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. MATLAB evaluates top to bottom and executes only the first matching branch.</p></feedback></choice>
					<choice><statement><p>All true branches execute.</p></statement><feedback><p>No; once a true branch is found, the rest are skipped.</p></feedback></choice>
					<choice><statement><p>The last matching branch executes.</p></statement><feedback><p>MATLAB takes the first match, not the last.</p></feedback></choice>
					<choice><statement><p>Both the matching branch and the else branch execute.</p></statement><feedback><p>The else branch only runs when no condition is true.</p></feedback></choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

        <task label="rq-if-trace-1">
            <statement>
				<p>What is printed by the following code?</p>
				<program language="matlab">x = 5;
					if x &gt; 10
						disp('big')
					elseif x &gt; 3
						disp('medium')
					else
						disp('small')
					end
				</program>
			</statement>
            <choices>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><c>medium</c></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. <pf>x = 5</pf> is not greater than 10, but is greater than 3.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><c>big</c></p></statement><feedback><p><pf>5 &gt; 10</pf> is false.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><c>small</c></p></statement><feedback><p>The <pf>else</pf> only runs when both conditions are false.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>Both <c>medium</c> and <c>small</c> are printed.</p></statement><feedback><p>Only the first true branch executes.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </task>

        <task label="rq-if-true-value">
            <statement correct="no"><p>In MATLAB, only the value <pf>1</pf> is treated as true in an if condition.</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>False. Any nonzero value is treated as true.</p></feedback>
        </task>

        <task label="rq-if-multiple-elseif">
            <statement><p>How many <pf>elseif</pf> clauses may appear in a single if statement?</p></statement>
            <choices>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>Any number.</p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. There is no limit on <pf>elseif</pf> clauses.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>At most one.</p></statement><feedback><p>You may chain as many <pf>elseif</pf> clauses as needed.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>At most two.</p></statement><feedback><p>No such limit exists.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>Zero.</p></statement><feedback><p><pf>elseif</pf> is optional but you may include many.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </task>

        <task label="rq-if-no-match">
            <statement><p>What happens if none of the conditions in an if/elseif chain are true and there is no <pf>else</pf> clause?</p></statement>
            <choices>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>No code in the if block executes; execution continues after <pf>end</pf>.</p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. The block is simply skipped.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>MATLAB throws an error.</p></statement><feedback><p>No error occurs; the block is just skipped.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>The first branch executes by default.</p></statement><feedback><p>Branches only execute when their condition is true.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>The last branch executes by default.</p></statement><feedback><p>Without an <pf>else</pf>, nothing runs if all conditions are false.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </task>

        <task label="rq-if-keyword">
            <statement><p>The correct MATLAB spelling for a secondary condition in an if chain is:</p></statement>
            <choices>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>elseif</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. Written as one word in MATLAB.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>else if</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Two words creates a nested if rather than an elseif chain.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>elif</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>That is Python syntax, not MATLAB.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>elsif</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>That is Ruby syntax, not MATLAB.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </task>

        <task label="rq-if-else-if-vs-elseif">
            <statement correct="no"><p><pf>else if</pf> (two words) and <pf>elseif</pf> (one word) are interchangeable in MATLAB.</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>False. <pf>else if</pf> opens a nested if block requiring its own <pf>end</pf>; <pf>elseif</pf> is part of the same block.</p></feedback>
        </task>

        <task label="rq-if-fill-end">
            <statement><p>Complete the if statement: <pf>if x &gt; 0 / disp('pos') / </pf><fillin answer="end" mode="string" width="6"/></p></statement>
        </task>

        <task label="rq-if-trace-2">
            <statement><p>What is the value of <pf>y</pf> after this code runs with <pf>x = -2</pf>?</p>
            <program language="matlab">x = -2;
if x &gt;= 0
    y = x;
else
    y = -x;
end</program></statement>
            <choices>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><c>2</c></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. The else branch assigns <pf>y = -(-2) = 2</pf> (absolute value).</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><c>-2</c></p></statement><feedback><p>The condition <pf>x &gt;= 0</pf> is false for <pf>x = -2</pf>, so the else runs.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><c>0</c></p></statement><feedback><p>Neither branch assigns 0.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>An error occurs.</p></statement><feedback><p>No error; the else branch handles negative values.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </task>

		</exercise>
	</reading-questions>

	<reading-questions xml:id="if-statement-coding-practice">
		<title>🧑🏻‍💻 Coding Practice</title>

		<introduction>
			<p>
				These coding activities focus on writing short MATLAB functions that use <pf>if</pf> statements.
			</p>
		</introduction>

		<exercise xml:id="if-statement-drills">
			<title><pf>if</pf>-statement Drills</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					Use the coding area to complete the each task. Some tasks build on the previous one, so copy and modify your code block as you go.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task xml:id="if-statement-drills-a">
				<title>Define <pf>y</pf></title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						After completing each part, click <kbd>Evaluate</kbd> to see the results. For parts 3 &amp; 4, click <kbd>Evaluate</kbd> multiple times to see how the results change based on the random value of <pf>x</pf>.
					</p>

					<tabular valign="top">
						<col width="30%"/>
						<col width="70%"/>
						<row>
							<cell><p><em>Part 1</em></p></cell>
							<cell><p>Define the variable <pf>x</pf> as <pf>6</pf>.</p></cell>
						</row>
						<row bottom="minor">
							<cell/>
							<cell><p>Define the variable <pf>y</pf> as <pf>1</pf> if <pf>x</pf> is less than or equal to <pf>9</pf>, otherwise set <pf>y</pf> to 4.</p></cell>
						</row>
						<row bottom="minor">
							<cell><p><em>Part 2</em></p></cell>
							<cell><p>Change <pf>x</pf> to <pf>16</pf>.</p></cell>
						</row>
						<row bottom="minor">
							<cell><p><em>Part 3</em></p></cell>
							<cell><p>Change <pf>x</pf> to be a random integer between -5 and 15 using <pf>randi([-5, 15])</pf>.</p></cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><p><em>Part 4</em></p></cell>
							<cell><p>Update your if statement so that it sets <pf>y</pf> to <pf>1</pf> if</p></cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell/>
							<cell><p><pf>x</pf> is between 1 and 9 (inclusive), otherwise set <pf>y</pf> to 4.</p></cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>

					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%

							%───────────────────────────%
							fprintf('for x = %d, we set y to %d\n', x, y);
						</input>
					</sage>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<title>Part 1</title>
					<program language="matlab">
						%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%
						x = 6;
						if x &lt;= 9
							y = 1;
						else
							y = 4;
						end
						%───────────────────────────%
						fprintf('for x = %d, we set y to %d\n', x, y);
					</program>
				</solution>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<title>Part 4</title>
					<program language="matlab">
						%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%
						x = randi([-5, 15]);
						if x &gt;= 1 &amp; x &lt;= 9
							y = 1;
						else
							y = 4;
						end
						%───────────────────────────%
						fprintf('for x = %d, we set y to %d\n', x, y);
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task xml:id="if-statement-drills-b">
				<title>Updating a Variable</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Suppose <pf>n</pf> is a random integer between 0 and 20. 
						Give the code that 
						<ul>
							<li>increases <pf>n</pf> by 5 if it is greater than 10,</li>
							<li>decreases <pf>n</pf> by 5 if it is less than 10, and</li>
							<li>sets <pf>n</pf> to 1 if it is equal to 10.</li>
						</ul>
					</p>

					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							n = randi([0, 20]);
							fprintf('Random number n = %d\n', n);
							%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%

							%───────────────────────────%
							fprintf('Updated n = %d\n', n);
						</input>
					</sage>
				</statement>

				<solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						<code>
							n = randi([0, 20]);
							fprintf('Starting n = %d\n', n);
							%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%
							if n &gt; 10
								n = n + 5;
							elseif n &lt; 10
								n = n - 5;
							else
								n = 1;
							end
							%───────────────────────────%
							fprintf('Updated n = %d\n', n);
						</code>
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="if-statement-activity-functions">
			<title>Writing Functions with if Statements</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					For each, write a function that uses if statements.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task xml:id="if-statement-activity-functions-sign-label-pt-1">
				<title>Sign Label (part 1)</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Create a function named <pf>sign_label</pf> that takes one input <pf>x</pf> and returns one output
						<pf>label</pf> with the value <pf>'positive'</pf>, <pf>'negative'</pf>, or <pf>'zero'</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				 <solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						function label = sign_label(x)
							if x &gt; 0
								label = 'positive';
							elseif x &lt; 0
								label = 'negative';
							else
								label = 'zero';
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution> 
			</task>

			<task xml:id="if-statement-activity-functions-sign-label-pt-2">
				<title>Sign Label (part 2)</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Modify the function so it returns a second output named <pf>code</pf> with values <pf>1</pf>, <pf>-1</pf>,
						or <pf>0</pf> that match the sign.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						function [label, code] = sign_label(x)
							if x &gt; 0
								label = 'positive';
								code = 1;
							elseif x &lt; 0
								label = 'negative';
								code = -1;
							else
								label = 'zero';
								code = 0;
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution> 
			</task>

			<task xml:id="if-statement-activity-functions-freeze-alert-pt-1">
				<title>Freeze Alert (part 1)</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a function named <pf>freeze_alert</pf> that takes <pf>tempC</pf> (a scalar temperature in
						Celsius) and <pf>tempWarn</pf> (how close to freezing to warn) and returns <pf>message</pf>. 
						If <pf>tempC</pf> is at or below <pf>tempWarn</pf>, return <pf>'Freeze warning'</pf>; otherwise return <pf>'No warning'</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				 <solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						function message = freeze_alert(tempC, tempWarn)
							if tempC &lt;= tempWarn
								message = 'Freeze warning';
							else
								message = 'No warning';
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution> 
			</task>

			<task xml:id="if-statement-activity-functions-freeze-alert-pt-2">
				<title>Freeze Alert (part 2)</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Update the function to return a second output <pf>isFreezing</pf> that is <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf>
						along with the message.
					</p>
				</statement>
				 <solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						function [message, isFreezing] = freeze_alert(tempC, tempWarn)

							if tempC &lt;= 0
								message = 'Freeze warning';
								isFreezing = true;
							elseif tempC &lt;= tempWarn
								message = 'Freeze warning';
								isFreezing = false;
							else
								message = 'No warning';
								isFreezing = false;
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution> 
			</task>

			<task xml:id="if-statement-activity-functions-ticket-price-pt-1">
				<title>Ticket Price (part 1)</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Create a function named <pf>ticket_price</pf> that takes a scalar input <pf>age</pf> (a single person's age, in years) and returns
						<pf>price</pf> using the rules below:
					</p>
					<ul marker="square">
						<li>Age less than 13: <pf>price = 6</pf></li>
						<li>Age from 13 to 64: <pf>price = 12</pf></li>
						<li>Age 65 or older: <pf>price = 8</pf></li>
					</ul>
				</statement>
				<solution component="solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						function price = ticket_price(age)
							if age &lt; 13
								price = 6;
							elseif age &lt; 65
								price = 12;
							else
								price = 8;
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task xml:id="if-statement-activity-functions-ticket-price-pt-2">
				<title>Ticket Price (part 2)</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Modify the function so it returns a second output <pf>group</pf> and handles invalid ages.
						If <pf>age &lt; 0</pf>, return <pf>price = NaN</pf> and <pf>group = 'invalid'</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						function [price, group] = ticket_price(age)
							if age &lt; 0
								price = NaN;
								group = 'invalid';
							elseif age &lt; 13
								price = 6;
								group = 'child';
							elseif age &lt; 65
								price = 12;
								group = 'adult';
							else
								price = 8;
								group = 'senior';
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task xml:id="if-statement-activity-functions-letter-grade-pt-1">
				<title>Letter Grade (part 1)</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a function named <pf>letter_grade</pf> that returns <pf>'A'</pf>, <pf>'B'</pf>, <pf>'C'</pf>,
						<pf>'D'</pf>, or <pf>'F'</pf> for a scalar <pf>score</pf> using the standard 90/80/70/60 cutoffs.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						function letter = letter_grade(score)
							if score &gt;= 90
								letter = 'A';
							elseif score &gt;= 80
								letter = 'B';
							elseif score &gt;= 70
								letter = 'C';
							elseif score &gt;= 60
								letter = 'D';
							else
								letter = 'F';
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task xml:id="if-statement-activity-functions-letter-grade-pt-2">
				<title>Letter Grade (part 2)</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Add an input check so that scores outside <pf>0</pf> to <pf>100</pf> return <pf>'invalid'</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						function letter = letter_grade(score)
							if score &lt; 0 || score &gt; 100
								letter = 'invalid';
							elseif score &gt;= 90
								letter = 'A';
							elseif score &gt;= 80
								letter = 'B';
							elseif score &gt;= 70
								letter = 'C';
							elseif score &gt;= 60
								letter = 'D';
							else
								letter = 'F';
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task xml:id="if-statement-activity-functions-letter-grade-pt-3">
				<title>Letter Grade (part 3)</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Concept check: why must the <pf>score &gt;= 90</pf> test appear before the <pf>score &gt;= 80</pf> test?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="solution">
					<p>
						Conditions are checked in order. If the <pf>score &gt;= 80</pf> test came first, scores of 90 or above
						would already satisfy it and never reach the <pf>A</pf> branch.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task xml:id="if-statement-activity-functions-late-fee-pt-1">
				<title>Late Fee (part 1)</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a function named <pf>late_fee</pf> that takes a single number <pf>daysLate</pf> (the number of days late) and returns <pf>fee</pf> according to the table below:
					</p>
					<tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
						<row>
							<cell><pf>daysLate</pf></cell><cell><pf>fee</pf></cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>0 or less</cell><cell>0</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>1 to 3</cell><cell>5</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>3 to 7</cell><cell>15</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>\quad</m> more than 7<m>\quad</m></cell><cell><m>\quad</m>30<m>\quad</m></cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
				</statement>
				<solution component="solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						function fee = late_fee(daysLate)
							if daysLate &lt;= 0
								fee = 0;
							elseif daysLate &lt;= 3
								fee = 5;
							elseif daysLate &lt;= 7
								fee = 15;
							else
								fee = 30;
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task xml:id="if-statement-activity-functions-late-fee-pt-2">
				<title>Late Fee (part 2)</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Modify the function to return a second output <pf>status</pf> with values according to the table below:
					</p>
					<tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
						<row>
							<cell><pf>daysLate</pf></cell><cell><pf>fee</pf></cell><cell><pf>status</pf></cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>0 or less</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>on time</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>1 to 3</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>minor late</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>3 to 7</cell><cell>15</cell><cell>late</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>\quad</m> more than 7<m>\quad</m></cell><cell><m>\quad</m>30<m>\quad</m></cell><cell><m>\quad</m>very late<m>\quad</m></cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
				</statement>
				<solution component="solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						function [fee, status] = late_fee(daysLate)
							if daysLate &lt;= 0
								fee = 0;
								status = 'on time';
							elseif daysLate &lt;= 3
								fee = 5;
								status = 'minor late';
							elseif daysLate &lt;= 7
								fee = 15;
								status = 'late';
							else
								fee = 30;
								status = 'very late';
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<exercise label="ex-if-practice-1">
			<statement>
				<p>What does the following code print when <pf>n = 0</pf>?</p>
				<program language="matlab">
					n = 0;
					if n > 0
					disp('positive')
					elseif n &lt; 0
					disp('negative')
					else
					disp('zero')
					end
				</program>
			</statement>
			<choices>
				<choice correct="yes"><statement><p><c>zero</c></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. Both conditions are false, so the else branch runs.</p></feedback></choice>
				<choice><statement><p><c>positive</c></p></statement><feedback><p><pf>0 &gt; 0</pf> is false.</p></feedback></choice>
				<choice><statement><p><c>negative</c></p></statement><feedback><p><pf>0 &lt; 0</pf> is also false.</p></feedback></choice>
				<choice><statement><p>Nothing is printed.</p></statement><feedback><p>The else branch runs when all conditions are false.</p></feedback></choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>
        <exercise label="pa-if-classify-number">
            <title>Classify a Number</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Write an if/elseif/else block that classifies <pf>x = 15</pf> as <c>positive</c>, <c>negative</c>, or <c>zero</c>. When done, evaluate and confirm it prints <c>positive</c>.</p>
                <sage language="octave">
                    <input>
x = 15;
% ==== Type your code here ====

                    </input>
                </sage>
            </statement>
            <solution component="example-solution">
                <program language="matlab">
x = 15;
if x > 0
    disp('positive')
elseif x &lt; 0
    disp('negative')
else
    disp('zero')
end
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="pa-if-grade">
            <title>Letter Grade</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Write an if/elseif/else block to assign a letter grade for <pf>score = 74</pf>: A (≥90), B (≥80), C (≥70), D (≥60), F (otherwise). Print the grade.</p>
                <sage language="octave">
                    <input>
score = 74;
% ==== Type your code here ====

                    </input>
                </sage>
            </statement>
            <solution component="example-solution">
                <program language="matlab">
score = 74;
if score >= 90
    grade = 'A';
elseif score >= 80
    grade = 'B';
elseif score >= 70
    grade = 'C';
elseif score >= 60
    grade = 'D';
else
    grade = 'F';
end
fprintf('Grade: %s\n', grade)
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="pa-if-max">
            <title>Larger of Two Numbers</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Write an if/else block that stores the larger of <pf>a = 17</pf> and <pf>b = 9</pf> in a variable <pf>larger</pf>, then prints it.</p>
                <sage language="octave">
                    <input>
a = 17;
b = 9;
% ==== Type your code here ====

                    </input>
                </sage>
            </statement>
            <solution component="example-solution">
                <program language="matlab">
a = 17;
b = 9;
if a > b
    larger = a;
else
    larger = b;
end
fprintf('larger = %g\n', larger)
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="pa-if-fizzbuzz">
            <title>FizzBuzz (Single Number)</title>
            <statement>
                <p>For <pf>n = 15</pf>, print <c>FizzBuzz</c> if divisible by both 3 and 5, <c>Fizz</c> if divisible by 3 only, <c>Buzz</c> if by 5 only, otherwise print the number. Use <pf>mod(n, k)</pf> to test divisibility.</p>
                <sage language="octave">
                    <input>
n = 15;
% ==== Type your code here ====

                    </input>
                </sage>
            </statement>
            <solution component="example-solution">
                <program language="matlab">
n = 15;
if mod(n,3) == 0 &amp;&amp; mod(n,5) == 0
    disp('FizzBuzz')
elseif mod(n,3) == 0
    disp('Fizz')
elseif mod(n,5) == 0
    disp('Buzz')
else
    disp(n)
end
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
